  "live_records": {
    "q-332876": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332876",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-332876-a",
