    "q-333795": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333795",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
