    "q-334638": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334638",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
